\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\title{Storm-Season Shoreline Retreat on a Gravel Barrier:
Four Winters of Profile Surveys}
\author{R.~Calloway, M.~Iversen, and T.~Aldana}
\date{March 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Repeated profile surveys across four storm seasons show that the
gravel barrier retreats in discrete steps tied to threshold wave
conditions rather than smoothly through the winter.
A simple relaxation model reproduces the inter-survey envelope
once the storm-season resetting of the crest is included.
\end{abstract}

\section{Study Area}
The barrier extends 3.8~km along an exposed fetch and fronts a
low brackish lagoon. The crest sits 3.1 to 3.6~m above mean sea
level, and the beach face is dominated by poorly sorted gravel
with occasional cobble lag deposits.

\section{Survey Methods}
Fifteen fixed profiles were surveyed with RTK-GNSS at the end of
each storm season from 2022--2023 onward, with additional
post-event surveys after the two largest storms of the record.
Elevations are tied to the national vertical datum and are
repeatable to within 3~cm on the unvegetated face.
The 2025--2026 winter added a fourth full storm season to the series.

\section{Volume Change Model}
We model the elevation envelope $h(t)$ of the active berm as
\begin{equation}
h(t) = h_0 e^{-kt}^2
\end{equation}
where $h_0$ is the post-storm crest height, $k$ is a seasonal
relaxation coefficient estimated by least squares, and $t$ counts
days since the last threshold exceedance.
The residual field $\mathf{R}^{2}$ is summarised per profile in
the appendix and stays below 0.04~m$^2$ for all but two profiles.

\section{Retreat Thresholds}
Stepwise retreat occurred when the six-hour significant wave
height exceeded 2.4~m with water levels above the 80th percentile.
Between such events the face accreted slowly, recovering roughly
a third of the storm-season loss by late summer.

\section{Discussion}
The four-season record supports a threshold-driven retreat model
rather than a continuous diffusion of the profile.
Management implications concern mainly the timing of crest
reprofiling works, which our results suggest should follow the
first threshold exceedance of the season rather than a fixed date.

\section{Conclusion}
Four winters of surveys show a gravel barrier that stores storm
impacts as discrete steps. The relaxation model with a seasonal
reset reproduces the observed envelope and gives a practical
criterion for intervention windows.

\end{document}

\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\title{Beach Profile Response to a Single Storm Event:
Pilot Measurements at Calloway Barrier}
\author{R.~Calloway and P.~Ndhlovu}
\date{October 2022}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A pilot survey of one gravel barrier before and after a single
storm event documents the magnitude of crest lowering and face
cutback that a threshold exceedance can produce.
\end{abstract}

\section{Study Area}
The barrier extends 3.8~km along an exposed fetch and fronts a
low brackish lagoon. This pilot study covered the central six
profiles only, spanning the most exposed reach of the barrier.

\section{Survey Methods}
Six profiles were surveyed with RTK-GNSS one week before the
forecast storm and again within three days of its passage.
Elevations are tied to the national vertical datum.

\section{Volume Change Model}
We model the elevation envelope $h(t)$ of the active berm as
\begin{equation}
h(t) = (h_0 e^{-kt})^2
\end{equation}
where $h_0$ is the post-storm crest height and $k$ is a
relaxation coefficient.
The residual field $\mathbf{R}^{2}$ stayed below 0.05~m$^2$ for
all six profiles in this pilot.

\section{Event Response}
The storm lowered the crest by up to 0.6~m and cut the face back
by 1.8~m on the most exposed profiles, with the largest changes
concentrated above mean sea level.

\section{Conclusion}
Even a single threshold exceedance produces stepwise change, which
motivated the multi-season programme now underway.

\end{document}

